    "id": "train-2089",
    "question": "Sophia ate 1/6 of her pie and she put the rest on the fridge. If the pie left in the fridge weighs 1200 grams, how many grams did Sophia eat?",
    "gold": "240",
    "answer": "If Sophia ate 1/6 of the pie, then 6/6 - 1/6 = 5/6 is left in the fridge.\nLet x be the pie's original weight.\nThe current weight of the pie can be described by 5x/6=1200 grams\nSo, 5x=7200.\nAnd, the original weight is x = <<1440=1440>>1440 grams.\nSo, Sophia ate 1440 grams original - 1200 grams after= <<1440-1200=240>>240 grams of pie.\n#### 240"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many grams of pie Sophia ate, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the total weight of the pie.\n2. Calculate the weight of the pie that Sophia ate.\n\nFirst, we know that the pie left in the fridge weighs 1200 grams and this is the remaining part of the pie after Sophia ate some of it. Since Sophia ate \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) of the pie, the remaining part of the pie is \\(\\frac{5}{6}\\) of the total weight of the pie. Therefore, we can set up the following equation to find the total weight of the pie:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{6} \\times \\text{Total weight of the pie} = 1200 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nTo find the total weight of the pie, we solve for the total weight by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of \\(\\frac{5}{6}\\), which is \\(\\frac{6}{5}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total weight of the pie} = 1200 \\text{ grams} \\times \\frac{6}{5} = 1440 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nNow that we know the total weight of the pie is 1440 grams, we can find out how much of the pie Sophia ate. Since Sophia ate \\(\\frac{1}{6}\\) of the pie, we calculate the weight of the pie she ate as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Weight of the pie Sophia ate} = \\frac{1}{6} \\times 1440 \\text{ grams} = 240 \\text{ grams}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of the pie that Sophia ate is \\(\\boxed{240}\\) grams.",
